===Etymology 2===
See {{m|en|compliment}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{misspelling of|en|compliment}}.
# {{obsolete spelling of|en|compliment}}.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet Q1-2|act=II|scene=i|sig=D2|sigend=D2|versoend=1|page=29|passage=Faine vvould I dvvell on forme, faine faine denie, / VVhat I haue ſpoke: but farevvell '''complements'''. / Doeſt thou loue me?}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost Q1|act=I|scene=i|sig=A4|page=6|passage=[O]ur Court you knovv is haunted / VVith a refined trauailer of Spaine, / {{...}} / A man of '''complements''' vvhom right and vvrong / Haue choſe as vmpier of their mutenie.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=VI|canto=X|stanza=23|page=486|passage=Theſe three {{quote-gloss|the {{w|Charities}} or Graces}} on men all gracious gifts beſtovv, / VVhich decke the body or adorne the mynde, / To make them louely or vvell fauoured ſhovv, / As comely carriage, entertainement kynde, / Svveete ſemblant, friendly offices that bynde, / And all the '''complements''' of curteſie: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|book=V|chapter=The Substance of Baptisme; the Rites or Solemnities therunto Belonging; and that the Substance thereof Being Kept, Other Things in Baptisme may Giue Place to Necessitie|section=58|page=130|passage=[I]f the caſe be ſuch as permitteth not baptiſme to haue the decent '''complements''' of baptiſme, better it vvere to inioy the body vvithout his furniture, then to vvait for this till the opportunitie of that for vvhich vve deſire it be loſt.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=III|scene=i|page=273|column=2|passage='Tvvas neuer merry vvorld, / Since lowly feigning vvas call'd '''complement''': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Marcellinus Holland Roman Historie|chapter=Annotations|book=18|page=452|passage=''Lvſtrum'' vvas the ſpace of foure yeares, after vvhich time complet there vvas a ſolemne revievv and ceſſing holden at Rome; and the citie, by a Sacrifice purged vvith ſundrie other ceremoniall '''complements''', almoſt duely obſerved in everie revolution of ſuch a tearme of yeares: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Camden Holland Britain|chapter=The Maners and Cvstomes of the Britans|page=31|passage=And euen at this day verily, it {{quote-gloss|magic}} is in Britaine highly honoured, vvhere the people are ſo vvholly deuoted unto it, and that vvith all '''complement''' of ceremonies, as a man vvould thinke the Perſians learned all their Magicke from them.}}
#* {{RQ:Fletcher Faithfull Shepheardesse|sig=&#91;K4&#93;|verso=1|page=81|passage=Baniſh all '''complement''' but ſingle truth. / From euery tongue, and euery Shepheards heart, / Let them vſe perſvvading, but no Art: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=I|scene=ii|page=280|column=2|passage={{...}} I met him / VVith cuſtomarie '''complement''', vvhen hee / VVafting his eyes to th' contrary, and falling / A Lippe of much contempt, ſpeedes from me, and / So leaues me, to conſider vvhat is breeding, / That changes thus his Manners.}}
#* {{RQ:Brome Joviall Crew|sig=F3|sigend=F3|versoend=1|page=38|passage=VVhat is an Eſtate / Of VVealth and Povver, ballanc'd vvith their Freedom, / But a meer load of outvvard '''complement'''?}}
#* {{RQ:Prynne Soveraigne Power|part=2|page=83|passage=[T]he royall aſſent to juſt, neceſſary, publike Bills, is in truth but a formall Ceremony or '''complement''' {{...}} beſtovved by the people upon Kings for their greater honour, vvith ''this limitation, that they muſt not deny it vvhen they of right require it to any juſt or neceſſary lavv''; not ſimply to make, but declare & confirm a lavv already made and paſſed by both houſes {{quote-gloss|of Parliament}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|lines=122–125|page=84|passage=[W]hat honour that, / but tedious vvaſt of time to ſit and hear / So many hollovv '''complements''' and lies, / Outlandiſh flatteries?}}
#* {{RQ:Walton Lives|edition=4th|chapter=Cotton|page=12|passage=And, by this act, the vvorld is taught to knovv, / That, the true friendſhip vve to merit ovve, / Is not diſcharg'd by '''complement''', and ſhovv.}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=LIV|fable=A Fox and a Sick Lyon|page=54|passage=A Certain ''Lyon'' that had got a Politique Fit of Sickneſs, {{...}} vvrote him {{quote-gloss|the fox}} VVord hovv Ill he vvas, and hovv Mighty Glad he ſhould be of his Company, upon the Score of Ancient Friendſhip and Acquaintance. The ''Fox'' return'd the '''Complement''' vvith a Thouſand Prayers for his Recovery; but as for VVaiting upon him, he deſir'd to be Excus'd; For (ſays he) I find the Traces of abundance of Feet Going In to Your Majeſties Palace, and not One that comes Back again.}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{misspelling of|en|compliment}}.
# {{obsolete spelling of|en|compliment}}.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The Coxcomb|act=I|scene=ii|page=98|column=1|passage=''Ser''{{quote-gloss|''vant''}}. Miſtris there are 2. Gentlemen. / ''Mar''{{quote-gloss|''ia''}}. VVhere? / ''Ser''. '''Complementing''' vvho ſhould firſt enter.}}
#* {{RQ:Andrewes Ninety-six Sermons|date=5 November 1617|part=8|chapter=A Sermon Preached before the King's Maiestie, at White-hall, on the V. of November, Anno Domini, MDCXVII|page=994|passage=It is a mock ſervice, as if vvhat ſerves man, vvould ſerve Him {{quote-gloss|[[w:God in Christianity|God]]}}: as if vve could '''complement''' it vvith {{smallcaps|God}}, vvith faces and phraſes, as vvith men vve do.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Areopagitica|page=8|passage=Sometimes 5 ''Imprimaturs'' are ſeen together dialogue-vviſe in the Piatza of one Title page, '''complementing''' and ducking each to other vvith their ſhav'n reverences, vvhether the Author, vvho ſtands by in perplexity at the foot of his Epiſtle, ſhall to the Preſſe or to the ſpunge.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Good Thoughts in Bad Times|section=VI|pages=141–142|pageref=141|passage={{...}} [[w:Artemidorus of Knidos|''Artemidorus'' {{quote-gloss|of Knidos}}]], [[w:Julius Caesar|{{quote-gloss|Julius}} ''Cæſar'']]'s friend, delivered him a Paper, (deſiring him to peruſe it,) vvherein the vvhole plot vvas diſcovered: But ''Cæſar'' '''complemented''' his life avvay, being ſo taken up, to return the ſalutations of ſuch People as met him in the vvay, that he pocketed the Paper, among other Petitions, as unconcerned therein, and ſo going to the Senate houſe, vvas ſlain.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Collected Sermons|volume=I|chapter=The Just Mans Funeral|section=paragraph 15|page=520|passage=Thus ''[[w:Rabshakeh|Rabſhakeh]]'' pretended a Commiſſion from [[w:God in Christianity|God]] for all the vvickedneſs he committed, and '''complements''' blaſphemie: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|book=VI|section=III|sectionname=To the Honourable, the Lady Mary Fountaine. [Of the Suppression of the Order of Observant Friers, and a Preparatory for the Dissolution of All the Rest.]|page=308|passage=Hereupon, in the year of our Lord 1534, the aforeſaid vvhole Order of ''Friers Obſervant'' vvere ſuppreſſed, and ''Auguſtine-Friers'' ſubſtituted in their places. Nor vvere theſe ''Obſervants'' (like the ''Canon-Regulars'' in the laſt Chapter) diſpoſed of in other Foundations, but totally and finally baniſhed out of all Religious Societies. For, King [[w:Henry VIII|''Henry'' {{quote-gloss|VIII}}]] his ſmiles '''complemented''' the former out of their Houſes, by their ovvn vvilling condeſcenſion; vvhilſt his frovvns outed theſe as Delinquents, by a violent expulſion.}}
#* {{RQ:Cockayne Small Poems|act=III|page=469|passage='''Complement''' vvith me no more then ''I'' '''complement''' vvith you; good Madam ſiſter ſit you dovvn, ''I'' vvould talk vvith you a little.}}
